e provided HTML, so I can't create selectors for those yet. Those will have to wait until after the login action is taken and the page updates.\n\nSo, the selectors I can provide now are for the username, password, and login button. Each with their respective confidence scores and reasoning.\n</think>\n\nHere is the structured response based on the analysis:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Username input field\",\n    \"selector\": \"#username\",\n    \"xpath\": \"//input[@id='username']\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.9,\n    \"reasoning\": \"The username input has a unique ID, making it a reliable selector.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Password input field\",\n    \"selector\": \"#password\",\n    \"xpath\": \"//input[@id='password']\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.9,\n    \"reasoning\": \"The password input has a unique ID, ensuring a stable selector.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Login button\",\n    \"selector\": \"#loginForm button[type='submit']\",\n    \"xpath\": \"//form[@id='loginForm']/button[@type='submit']\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.85,\n    \"reasoning\": \"Targeting the submit button within the form by type ensures we get the correct button.\"\n  }\n]"}
